From 786cdae38050b04cc3e3a00e71546ce949b9e50a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Aditya Pulipaka <adipu@utexas.edu>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 16:57:36 -0700
Subject: [PATCH 4/4] Metal: fixed-size arrays (wp.zeros in kernels);
 thread-qualify reference casts in native snippets

MSL has no derived classes and its address spaces are disjoint, so the CPU/CUDA
fixedarray_t (array_t subclass over a stack array) cannot exist on Metal: array_t's
data is a device pointer and no cast reaches thread memory. On Metal a fixed array is
now a plain array_t over a per-thread slice of a device scratch buffer:

- array.h: fixedarray_t<Size, T> aliases array_t<T>; fixedarray_zeros<Size, T, Offset>
  zero-fills and views the slot, fixedarray_copy gives `b = a` value semantics.
- codegen: each wp.zeros()/fixed-array copy site owns a static 16-byte aligned slot in
  its function's frame (adj.fixed_frame_bytes, declared as _wp_fixed_frame). Generated
  functions take the thread's scratch as a hidden argument next to the tile arena
  (WP_FUNC_PARAM/WP_FUNC_ARG/WP_FUNC_CALL in tile.h, advanced past the caller's frame per
  call); tile builtins keep WP_TILE_ARENA_*. fixed_stack_bytes() sizes a kernel's stack
  (own frame + deepest callee chain) and the Metal entry points read the scratch address
  after the arguments (wp_fixed_args_<kernel>) when it is nonzero.
- context: the stride is stored in the module meta (<kernel>_fixed_bytes) and on
  KernelHooks; invoke_metal appends a grow-only scratch buffer's address (translated like
  any array pointer, so graph capture works) and keeps outgrown buffers alive.

Because the storage is device memory, .ptr and wp.array(ptr=...) views of it work, which
Newton's multicontact manifold builder (GJK/MPR narrow phase) relies on. Difference from
CPU/CUDA: a fixed array returned from a function lives in the callee's frame.

Also: @wp.func_native snippets compiled for Metal get `thread` on unqualified
reference casts (reinterpret_cast<uint32_t&>(f) in Newton's float_flip), and
codegen_snippet receives the device from the module builder.

Tests: warp.tests.test_fixedarray 20/20 (CPU + Metal, incl. capture and ptr);
test_func, test_grad, tile.test_tile, tile.test_tile_func_arg pass on Metal. Newton's
mesh/convex narrow phase compiles on Metal and matches CPU (box, hull, mesh on plane;
hull on box/hull: identical contact counts, distances and resting heights).

